    "slot_number": "slot-4-2-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Order status progression and manual review handling",
    "slot_value": "After submission, the order should start as pending while payment and availability are checked. If everything is fine, it can move to confirmed quickly. If there is a payment problem, stock issue, or anything needing manual review, it should stay pending or move to a hold or rejected state instead of confirming right away. During manual review, the customer should see that the order is received but not yet finalized and waiting for approval. Manual review should consider suspicious payment activity, mismatched customer and shipping details, unusual order size, policy exceptions staff need to catch before fulfillment, high-value orders, mismatched customer details, and repeated failed payment attempts. If approved, the customer should be told the order is confirmed and moving ahead; if kept on hold, they should be told it is still under review and no action may be needed yet; if rejected, they should be told the order cannot be completed and may need to try again or contact support. Customer messaging should stay high level and avoid exposing internal fraud or security details. If staff can’t verify an issue quickly, keep the order on hold first rather than deciding too early. If the missing information must come from the customer, send the order back for customer action with clear instructions on what to update or send in. If it is an internal issue that still needs investigation, keep it on hold until staff can approve or reject it. It should only move into fulfillment when payment is fully accepted, the order is no longer under review, and the items are confirmed available to ship. If any item is out of stock, payment is unresolved, or a staff review is still open, it should wait and not go to picking and packing yet. Fulfillment should start only when there is no blocker left.",
